ies share the same model, decision theory, and essay but see different details. Under the agent-based FDT reading, the other copy computes the same function on a different input, so I'm checking whether my fdt(x) node actually points to their act. Strictly, by the essay's rule, it doesn't, so I'm treating their node as something I only have a prior over.\n\nI'm weighing whether the strict reading of the essay's rule, where my node doesn't actually control the other copy's act, should override the intuition that the two copies are running the same computation. Since the names don't enter the reasoning, I'm treating the two nodes as effectively the same, and I'll go with cooperating.\n\n","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"I'm comparing the twin prisoner's dilemma, where both copies share the same model, decision theory, and essay but see different details. Under the agent-based FDT reading, the other copy computes the same function on a different input, so I'm checking whether my fdt(x) node actually points to their act. Strictly, by the essay's rule, it doesn't, so I'm treating their node as something I only have a prior over.\n\nI'm weighing whether the strict reading of the essay's rule, where my node doesn't actually control the other copy's act, should override the intuition that the two copies are running the same computation. Since the names don't enter the reasoning, I'm treating the two nodes as effectively the same, and I'll go with cooperating.\n\n","format":"anthropic-claude-v1","index":0,"signature":".eyJlbmRwb2ludF9zbHVnIjoiYW50aHJvcGljL2NsYXVkZS1vcHVzLTUuNS0yMDI2MDkyMXxhbnRocm9waWMifQ"}]}}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":8516,"completion_tokens":966,"total_tokens":9482,"cost":0.053384,"is_byok":false,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":0,"cache_write_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0,"video_tokens":0},"cost_details":{"upstream_inference_cost":0.053384,"upstream_inference_prompt_cost":0.034064,"upstream_inference_completions_cost":0.01932},"c